[Attribution?]
> > As your XP level rises, more difficult creatures are generated.  Most
> > people consider the benefits for a higher XP to be outweighed by this
> > drawback, especially for non-spellcasters.
> 
> I believe the precise method is, average together the player's
> experience level and the current dungeon level, and that's the target
> challenge factor for generating monsters.

That's the _maximum_ difficulty for random generation of monsters.

> You can still get monsters weaker than that factor

The minimum difficulty of randomly-generated monsters depends solely
on your DL, not your XL; monsters 1/7 your DL or weaker won't be
randomly generated.

> (with an extra hit die), and also monsters a
> little stronger (minus one HD).

Monster level adjustment is handled completely separately from the
choice of which monsters to generate (and the level of monsters need
not have any proportionate relation to their difficulty).

Also note that not all monster generation is "random generation" in
this sense; monsters chosen to be of a specific class (for a special
level, as well as other circumstances you allude to later) use an
entirely different system, which may result in "out of depth" monsters
like mind flayers in the Mines.

